# Does increased time preference lead to higher BMI? — NLSY79 (data/nls_raw.csv)

## Hypothesis (data-grounded)
**Higher present-oriented financial behaviour is associated with higher adiposity, but the
association is only partly prospective and is heavily attenuated by prior weight — the data
support a positive *association* between savings-based present orientation and BMI, not a
demonstrated causal effect of time preference on BMI.**

Operationalised with the only preference-relevant fields in the file — the 1989 items
"Was more money put into or taken out of R/spouse savings since last interview, 1989"
(1 put in / 3 no change / 2 took more out / 4 no savings, ordered 0<1<2<3 as a
**savings-based present-orientation proxy**, not a measured discount rate), its binary form
TP_high = took-more-out or no-savings, and a continuous net-saving/income ratio — the proxy is
positively graded with BMI1989 (mean BMI 24.54 saver → 24.97 no change → 25.14 dis-saver →
25.50 no savings; obesity 10.1% → 11.6% → 13.0% → 15.6%; r = +0.077, n = 10,410), and the
gradient survives adjustment for age, sex, race cohort, ASVAB cognitive score, parental
education and occupation, family size, 1985 family net wealth and 1989 family income
(TP-ordinal b = +0.230 BMI units per step, SE 0.061, p = 1.6e-4, n = 4,569; TP_high
b = +0.436, p = 0.001; obesity OR 1.10 per step). Direction is NOT established: holding
eight-year-prior BMI1981 fixed erases 54–58% of the coefficient (to +0.096, p = 0.020), the
genuinely prospective BMI1992|BMI1989 model shrinks to +0.063 per step (p = 0.082, n = 3,861),
and baseline BMI1981 itself predicts *higher* present orientation in 1989 (b = +0.013 per BMI
unit, p = 0.003) — i.e. earlier adiposity predicts later saving behaviour at least as clearly
as the reverse. The relation is concentrated in women (unadjusted r = +0.133 vs +0.008 in men;
adjusted +0.299 vs +0.170), and the largest single contrast is the 'no savings' stratum
(+0.72 vs savers), which is also the poorest group (median 1985 wealth $300 vs $4,300); that
stratification is why the 3-category (no-savings dropped, +0.209, p = 0.009) and the
dis-saver-vs-saver contrast (+0.42, p = 0.009) are reported — both stay positive and
significant, so the finding is not driven by the 'no savings' group alone, but once prior BMI
is controlled neither the dis-saver (+0.12, p = 0.22) nor the no-savings (+0.16, p = 0.21)
contrast remains significant.

**Pre-registered expected sign: POSITIVE (more present-oriented → higher BMI). Matches in
54/59 fitted specifications** (the 5 exceptions are all the 'no change' category dummy, which
is slightly *below* savers and never significant); every primary exposure (TP-ordinal, TP_high,
TP 3-category, dis-saver, no-savings, and the continuous net-saving ratio with its expected
negative sign) matches in every design.

