## Unit decodes (metadata is wrong; verified in data)
* weight columns are **pounds** (median 140/158/165; sex means 161/129 lb in 1981), not kg → ×0.45359.
* `Height of respondent, 1981` is **feet×100 + inches** (range 400–611; (v mod 100) never exceeds 11);
  `Height 1985` is plain inches (48–81). Decoded 1981 mean 67.0 in, 1985 mean 67.1 in, r = 0.934,
  median difference 0 in. BMI uses the **person-mean** of the two decoded heights.
* ASVAB "Z scores" are ~500/100 standard scores (mean 476, SD 100) → re-standardised.

## Sample, trimming, missing data
12,686 respondents (one row per ID, 0 duplicates). Value-level invalidation (not row deletion):
height 1981 out of 52–80 in → 11; height 1985 → 8; weight 1981 → 1; weight 1989 → 11; weight 1992 → 0;
BMI out of 12–70 → 1/11/4. Sentinels/top-codes → missing: put-in 8,192,797 (10), took-out 9,999,996 (20);
income-1989 top-code 1,057,448 (167) winsorised before the net-saving ratio (winsor bounds −0.645/+0.395).
Retention ladder: 12,686 → 10,410 (exposure + usable BMI1989) → 9,012 (core controls) → 6,875
(+income/wealth) → 4,569 (full set); 1992 model n = 3,861 (37.1% of the 1989 pool).
Structural zeros kept for savings codes 3/4 (n = 4,785); 445 item-non-response codes 1/2 with a
sentinel/absent dollar amount stay missing. Sensitivity: missing-indicator + median imputation and
stochastic chained-equation imputation of **controls only** (M = 10, n = 10,410) give
+0.166 (SD across imputations 0.007) — between the listwise full-adjusted +0.230 and the
prior-BMI-adjusted +0.096; exposure and outcomes are never imputed.
Attrition: savings wave present for 80.9% of men / 85.9% of women, 88.2% Black / 86.9% Hispanic /
80.6% non-black non-Hispanic; 1992 weight present for 95.3% Black / 93.1% Hispanic / 71.5% non-black
non-Hispanic, so the 1992 model over-represents the non-black/non-Hispanic cohort (63.8% vs 57.3% in
the pool); cohort post-stratification weights (0.90/1.38/0.97) leave the coefficient at +0.065 (p = 0.077).
